{"name":"Graveyard Earth","hex":"#68553a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"graveyard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tract of land in which the dead are buried.","A final storage place for collections of things that are no longer useful or useable.\n\n The discard pile, in some trading card games.\n\n A team where players are sent when they are not useful, or a team where players become useless if sent there."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/graveyard"},{"term":"earth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Earth (“the third planet of the Solar System, upon which humans live”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Soil.","Any general rock-based material."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/earth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cemetery","blocks":[{"text":"A cemetery, burial ground, gravesite, graveyard, or a green space called a memorial park or memorial garden, is a place where the remains of many dead people are buried or otherwise entombed. The word cemetery implies that the land is specifically designated as a burial ground and originally applied to the Roman catacombs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Kerepesi_temet%C5%91_Budapest.jpg/330px-Kerepesi_temet%C5%91_Budapest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cemetery","wikidata":"Q39614"},{"title":"Earth","blocks":[{"text":"Earth is the third planet from the Sun and the only astronomical object known to harbor life. This is made possible by Earth being an ocean world, the only one in the Solar System sustaining liquid surface water. Almost all of Earth's water is contained in its ocean, which covers 70.8% of Earth's crust. The remaining 29.2% of Earth's crust is dry land, which is predominantly located within Earth's land hemisphere in the form of continental landmasses. Most of Earth's land is at least somewhat humid and covered by vegetation, while large ice sheets at Earth's polar deserts retain more water than Earth's groundwater, lakes, rivers, and atmospheric water combined. Earth's crust consists of slowly moving tectonic plates, which interact to produce mountain ranges, volcanoes, and earthquakes. Earth has a liquid outer core that generates a magnetosphere capable of deflecting most of the destructive solar winds and cosmic radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Meteosat-12-fci-march-equinox-2025-noon.jpg/330px-Meteosat-12-fci-march-equinox-2025-noon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth","wikidata":"Q2"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Cemetery_in_China.jpg/500px-Cemetery_in_China.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Cemetery_in_China.jpg/1280px-Cemetery_in_China.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Cemetery_in_China.jpg/500px-Cemetery_in_China.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cemetery in China","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACemetery_in_China.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Cemetery_in_Kavala%2C_Greece.jpg/500px-Cemetery_in_Kavala%2C_Greece.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Cemetery_in_Kavala%2C_Greece.jpg/1280px-Cemetery_in_Kavala%2C_Greece.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Cemetery_in_Kavala%2C_Greece.jpg/500px-Cemetery_in_Kavala%2C_Greece.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_c